# data/traces/*.jsonl are append-only logs, and dental_agent/training/git_sync.py
# (used by scripts/run_trace_gen.py's --git-sync-every) relies on that: multiple
# parallel Colab/Kaggle workers, each assigned a disjoint slice of image IDs
# (see --total-slices/--slice-index/--slice-seed), each append lines for
# DIFFERENT image_ids to the SAME shared file and sync back concurrently.
#
# Verified by hand (see git_sync.py's module docstring) that WITHOUT this
# line, git's default merge strategy reports a spurious conflict even for
# two workers' genuinely non-overlapping appends -- both sides modify the
# same "end of file" region relative to the same common ancestor, and a
# plain 3-way merge can't tell "these are just two independent appends,
# keep both" from a real conflict. `merge=union` is git's own built-in
# named merge driver for exactly this shape of file (take the union of
# both sides' added lines, no manual resolution, no external driver script
# needed) -- this is not a homegrown workaround.
#
# This does NOT catch a duplicate image_id ending up in the file twice
# (e.g. from operator error -- two workers accidentally given the same
# --slice-index): union merge has no concept of what a "duplicate" means
# for this file's content, it just unions lines. That's a real, separate
# risk this attribute doesn't address -- see roadmap.md's entry on
# git_sync.py for how that's handled instead (a duplicate-image_id check,
# not relying on git to catch it).
data/traces/*.jsonl merge=union
